{
  "_readme": "How a character's sheet words become a designed body (npcgen/bodymatch.py). `builds`: each entry gives the proportions (-1..1) a build word asks for; a sheet's build is read word by word and the entries it names are added together, so 'tall and lanky' is `tall` plus `lanky`. The size of one step was measured on the Body Designer's bodies (2026-10-07): height +-1 is about +-20 cm of the whole body; fat +-1 changes the volume by 6 to 10 per cent (a waist of 2.5 cm); muscle is definition, not bulk (+1 is defined and lean, -1 soft); chest +1 is 3 to 4 cm; hips +-1 is 3 cm; shoulders -1.6 / +3 cm. `skin`, `eyes` and `hair`: phrases (longest match first), then single colour words with modifiers; a word that is nowhere here is left out and said so, never guessed. `dyed` are the hair colours of a dyed head (the hair itself takes them, the brows never do); `hair_styles` says which of the Body Designer's game hair a sheet's hair text is (`crop`, `sweep` or `long`), whether it is bald (`none`), or names a style that has none yet (`other`: it wins over the rest, so 'short ponytail' is not a crop). `long` are the words for long hair: a text with one of them and none of `not_long` (up, short or middling: a bun, a bob, shoulder length), `crop`, `none` or `long_near` is the Body Designer's Long, whatever its colour or curl (the Long is straight and smooth, and the page says so); one that also says what the Long is not (`long_near`: braided, a ponytail, tied back, swept over a shoulder, curtain bangs) gets it as the nearest, said so.",
